lesson g5-circles v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=5 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Circles and the distance round them"
  goal "Name the radius and diameter of a circle, and find the distance round it as about 3.14 times the diameter."

  look {
    scene 26x16 {
      circleparts c size=5 lines=3 radius=5 diameter=10 around=31.4 unit="m" at=canvas(1, 1)
      observatory dome open=1 right-of=c gap=2
    }
    say """
      The observatory's dome stands on a circle. The radius goes from the centre to the edge, and
      the diameter goes right across through the centre, so it is two radii. The distance round a
      circle is its circumference. However big the circle, the circumference is always a little more
      than three diameters. How many exactly is a number called pi, written π, which is about 3.14.
      A dome 10 m across is about 31.4 m round.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice circle.across-the-dome count=1 seed=3
    level easy {
      worked circle.round-the-dome v=8 from="diameter" level=medium
    }
    practice circle.round-the-dome count=2 seed=4
    practice circle.rope-to-diameter count=1 seed=5
    practice circle.wheel-turns count=1 seed=6
    practice circle.wider-dome count=1 seed=2
    practice circle.arch-window count=1 seed=7 hard-count=2
    practice circle.across-the-dome count=1 seed=8
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show circle.radius-mistake r=5 easy-r=3 hard-r=7
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show circle.rope-a-metre-out r=8 w=1 easy-r=5 hard-r=20 hard-w=2
  }
  remember {
    say "The diameter is two radii. The circumference, the distance round, is pi times the diameter, and pi is about 3.14."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Wrap a string round a plate or a tin and lay it along the diameter: it goes a little more than
    three times. That is the idea, and 3.14 is its value. The common slip is multiplying the radius
    by 3.14, which gives only half the way round, so ask every time whether the line drawn is a
    radius or a diameter. Going the other way, the diameter is the distance round divided by 3.14.
    In the two-star question the reason is that 3.14 counts diameters. In the three-star question
    the extra length does not depend on the size of the dome at all, which surprises most adults
    too. If the child is unsure which line is which, stay here another day. Off the page, measure
    round and across three round things at home and divide one by the other.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the radius is given and the diameter asked, a dome 8 m across is worked once in the teacher's pen before domes 2, 3 or 5 m across, the platform is 10 or 20 m across, the wheels turn ten times, the big dome is twice as wide, the half-round windows are 2 or 4 m, Mo's mistake is with a radius of 3, and the three-star path is 1 m further out round a dome of radius 5."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the diameter is given and the radius asked, with halves, the rails are found from the radius of domes 5 to 12 m, the platform is 2, 4 or 5 m across, the wheels are 60 cm and turn three times, the big dome is three or four times as wide, the windows are 6 or 10 m and asked twice, Mo's mistake is with a radius of 7, and the three-star path is 2 m further out round a dome of radius 20."
  }
}
